Relevant columns and why they matter  
primary_type  : identify only “MOTOR VEHICLE THEFT” incidents  
date          : pull the calendar month with EXTRACT(MONTH FROM date) or FORMAT_DATE()  
year          : quick filter to 2016 instead of computing year(date) every time  
unique_key    : simple row identifier when we just want to view raw records  
description   : optional sanity‑check that we are indeed looking at auto‑theft‐related codes  

Typical conditions we will reuse  
• year = 2016  
• primary_type = 'MOTOR VEHICLE THEFT'  

Below are ten exploratory → analytic queries (all capped with LIMIT 20 so they run quickly while you explore). Replace PROJECT / DATASET / TABLE exactly as shown.

```sql
-- 1. Peek at a few motor‑vehicle‑theft rows in any year
SELECT unique_key, date, primary_type, description
FROM `bigquery-public-data.chicago_crime.crime`
WHERE primary_type = 'MOTOR VEHICLE THEFT'
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 2. Same peek but restricted to 2016 only
SELECT unique_key, date, primary_type, description
FROM `bigquery-public-data.chicago_crime.crime`
WHERE primary_type = 'MOTOR VEHICLE THEFT'
  AND year = 2016
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 3. Count how many motor‑vehicle‑theft records exist in 2016 overall
SELECT COUNT(*) AS mv_thefts_2016
FROM `bigquery-public-data.chicago_crime.crime`
WHERE primary_type = 'MOTOR VEHICLE THEFT'
  AND year = 2016
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 4. Pull distinct months (yyyy-mm) that have at least one such incident in 2016
SELECT DISTINCT FORMAT_DATE('%Y-%m', DATE(date)) AS yr_mo
FROM `bigquery-public-data.chicago_crime.crime`
WHERE primary_type = 'MOTOR VEHICLE THEFT'
  AND year = 2016
ORDER BY yr_mo
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 5. Simple month‑level counts, unsorted
SELECT FORMAT_DATE('%Y-%m', DATE(date)) AS yr_mo,
       COUNT(*) AS incidents
FROM `bigquery-public-data.chicago_crime.crime`
WHERE primary_type = 'MOTOR VEHICLE THEFT'
  AND year = 2016
GROUP BY yr_mo
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 6. Same as #5 but sorted descending by incidents
SELECT FORMAT_DATE('%Y-%m', DATE(date)) AS yr_mo,
       COUNT(*) AS incidents
FROM `bigquery-public-data.chicago_crime.crime`
WHERE primary_type = 'MOTOR VEHICLE THEFT'
  AND year = 2016
GROUP BY yr_mo
ORDER BY incidents DESC
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 7. Add EXTRACT(MONTH) and EXTRACT(YEAR) columns for clarity
SELECT EXTRACT(YEAR  FROM date)  AS yr,
       EXTRACT(MONTH FROM date)  AS mo,
       COUNT(*) AS incidents
FROM `bigquery-public-data.chicago_crime.crime`
WHERE primary_type = 'MOTOR VEHICLE THEFT'
  AND year = 2016
GROUP BY yr, mo
ORDER BY incidents DESC
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 8. Use a window to show each month’s count and the maximum across all months
WITH monthly AS (
  SELECT FORMAT_DATE('%Y-%m', DATE(date)) AS yr_mo,
         COUNT(*) AS incidents
  FROM `bigquery-public-data.chicago_crime.crime`
  WHERE primary_type = 'MOTOR VEHICLE THEFT'
    AND year = 2016
  GROUP BY yr_mo
)
SELECT yr_mo,
       incidents,
       MAX(incidents) OVER() AS max_for_2016
FROM monthly
ORDER BY incidents DESC
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 9. Isolate the single month that has the maximum number of incidents
SELECT yr_mo, incidents
FROM (
  SELECT FORMAT_DATE('%Y-%m', DATE(date)) AS yr_mo,
         COUNT(*) AS incidents,
         RANK() OVER(ORDER BY COUNT(*) DESC) AS rnk
  FROM `bigquery-public-data.chicago_crime.crime`
  WHERE primary_type = 'MOTOR VEHICLE THEFT'
    AND year = 2016
  GROUP BY yr_mo
)
WHERE rnk = 1
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 10. FINAL answer: highest number of motor‑vehicle‑theft incidents in any single 2016 month
SELECT MAX(monthly_count) AS highest_monthly_total_2016
FROM (
  SELECT COUNT(*) AS monthly_count
  FROM `bigquery-public-data.chicago_crime.crime`
  WHERE primary_type = 'MOTOR VEHICLE THEFT'
    AND year = 2016
  GROUP BY FORMAT_DATE('%Y-%m', DATE(date))
)
LIMIT 20;
```

